<!-- SCE Forge conformance: Zenoh linkstate-peer routing — the
     LinkStateList topology-exchange payload (a batch of LinkState
     entries, one per node the sender knows about).

     P4 routing track, linkstate port step (b): the body a peer floods
     to share its view of the mesh graph. Mirrors zenoh
     `zenoh/src/net/protocol/linkstate.rs:154-157` (the `LinkStateList`
     struct) + `zenoh/src/net/codec/linkstate.rs:146-182` (the
     `Zenoh080Routing` WCodec/RCodec):

         codec.write(&mut *writer, x.link_states.len())?;  // VLE count
         for ls in x.link_states.iter() {
             self.write(&mut *writer, ls)?;                 // each LinkState
         }

     Wire shape:
       byte 0+ : num_link_states  (VLE count)
       repeat  : link_states      (count * linkstate.scxml)

     The `|X|X|X|LK_ST_LS|` header byte in the zenoh ASCII diagram
     (linkstate.rs:148-153) is NOT part of this codec body — the
     `Zenoh080Routing` list codec writes only count + entries. That id
     byte belongs to the OAM / NetworkMessage carrier that frames this
     payload onto the wire (net/protocol/network.rs:357 writes the bare
     LinkStateList into a buffer that the OAM envelope then wraps); the
     carrier is linkstate port step (c). This codec is the body, exactly
     as the `hello` body codec carries no scouting-message HELLO id byte.

     Composition: B1-alpha VLE count + B5-mu repeat<codec> (ungated,
     count co-located). No new primitive surface. -->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="codec" sce:default-endian="big" name="linkstate_list">
  <sce:import src="linkstate.scxml" kind="codec" as="codec_zenoh_linkstate"/>
  <datamodel>
    <sce:field id="num_link_states" sce:type="uint64" sce:byte="0" sce:bit-size="vle"/>
    <sce:repeat id="link_states" type="codec_zenoh_linkstate" sce:byte="1"
                count="num_link_states" max-count="64"/>
  </datamodel>
</scxml>
